00:01:52.400 --> 00:01:54.066
MELANIE WARRICK: Great
Great, thank you.

00:01:54.066 --> 00:01:55.640
So I know you both
co-wrote a blog

00:01:55.640 --> 00:01:58.880
post that was helping to outline
what these vulnerabilities are.

00:01:58.880 --> 00:02:01.070
But can you tell us a
little bit more about them?

00:02:01.070 --> 00:02:01.820
MATT LINTON: Sure.

00:02:01.820 --> 00:02:04.100
So essentially, the core
of the vulnerability

00:02:04.100 --> 00:02:07.340
is something that has been
latent in CPUs for a long time,

00:02:07.340 --> 00:02:09.530
probably close to 20 years.

00:02:09.530 --> 00:02:12.380
And it exists in sort
of an efficiency trick

00:02:12.380 --> 00:02:15.530
that CPUs have been pulling
to get faster and faster.

00:02:15.530 --> 00:02:19.280
What happens is, when you
make a request of the CPU,

00:02:19.280 --> 00:02:21.710
and there's a couple of
different potential answers

00:02:21.710 --> 00:02:25.010
that may result, sometimes
the CPU will start

00:02:25.010 --> 00:02:27.320
executing all of
the instructions

00:02:27.320 --> 00:02:30.820
and preparing the results,
so that when you ask it

00:02:30.820 --> 00:02:33.092
for the specific
answer you wanted,

00:02:33.092 --> 00:02:35.300
it's already got the answer
ready to go and give you,

00:02:35.300 --> 00:02:37.220
instead of having to wait
for you to instruct it.

00:02:37.220 --> 00:02:38.750
And it turns out
that there are ways

00:02:38.750 --> 00:02:40.910
that someone can take
advantage of this

00:02:40.910 --> 00:02:44.660
by tricking the CPU
into executing branches

00:02:44.660 --> 00:02:47.690
against areas of memory
that would not ordinarily

00:02:47.690 --> 00:02:49.610
be accessible to
that person and then

00:02:49.610 --> 00:02:52.220
retrieving the results from
the speculated-- they call

00:02:52.220 --> 00:02:53.750
it the speculative branches.

00:02:53.750 --> 00:02:54.830
PAUL TURNER: Yeah,
I just wanted to add

00:02:54.830 --> 00:02:56.788
that I thought the way
that Matt describes this

00:02:56.788 --> 00:02:59.390
as tricking the CPU was a really
good way to think about it.

00:02:59.390 --> 00:03:01.550
Generally speaking,
the CPU is trying

00:03:01.550 --> 00:03:04.400
to do what it thinks should
happen next in your program.

00:03:04.400 --> 00:03:06.110
But these attacks
have discovered

00:03:06.110 --> 00:03:09.650
new ways of tricking it into
thinking that something should

00:03:09.650 --> 00:03:11.930
happen which wouldn't
normally happen

00:03:11.930 --> 00:03:13.610
during correct execution.

00:03:13.610 --> 00:03:16.580
And the way that that occurs
allows some protection

00:03:16.580 --> 00:03:17.910
domains to be bypassed.

00:03:17.910 --> 00:03:18.620
MELANIE WARRICK:
So what are things

00:03:18.620 --> 00:03:20.750
that are vulnerable to
this type of attack?

00:03:20.750 --> 00:03:23.780
MATT LINTON: Well, unlike a
lot of really dangerous attacks

00:03:23.780 --> 00:03:26.690
that we've seen before,
sort of the good news first

00:03:26.690 --> 00:03:30.390
is that this vulnerability
is a read-only vulnerability.

00:03:30.390 --> 00:03:33.200
The really core danger
here is that someone

00:03:33.200 --> 00:03:36.080
can trick the CPU
into giving you access

00:03:36.080 --> 00:03:37.910
to areas of system memory.

00:03:37.910 --> 00:03:40.287
Now that's very
important, because we

00:03:40.287 --> 00:03:41.870
have a lot of secrets
in system memory

00:03:41.870 --> 00:03:43.078
that you want to keep secret.

00:03:43.078 --> 00:03:45.500
You have passwords,
and encryption keys,

00:03:45.500 --> 00:03:46.560
and pretty much--

00:03:46.560 --> 00:03:47.300
PAUL TURNER: Credit
card numbers.

00:03:47.300 --> 00:03:48.050
MATT LINTON: Credit
card numbers.

00:03:48.050 --> 00:03:50.674
Any kind of data that you would
normally be processing and care

00:03:50.674 --> 00:03:52.460
about will touch memory.

00:03:52.460 --> 00:03:54.170
But unlike previous
vulnerabilities

00:03:54.170 --> 00:03:57.370
such as Heartbleed, an attacker
can't just reach out, touch

00:03:57.370 --> 00:03:58.626
you, and you're done.

00:03:58.626 --> 00:04:00.500
There already has to be
away for the attacker

00:04:00.500 --> 00:04:03.110
to get onto the system, in order
to exploit this vulnerability

00:04:03.110 --> 00:04:04.454
to steal secrets.

00:04:04.454 --> 00:04:05.870
Now I don't want
to minimize that.

00:04:05.870 --> 00:04:07.670
Because this is the
security industry.

00:04:07.670 --> 00:04:10.580
And every good pen tester
knows there are hundreds

00:04:10.580 --> 00:04:12.860
of ways to get onto a system.

00:04:12.860 --> 00:04:16.435
But it's not once and you're
dead like Heartbleed was.

00:04:16.435 --> 00:04:17.810
MELANIE WARRICK:
Well, and I read

00:04:17.810 --> 00:04:21.709
that this is impacting personal
computers, mobile phones,

00:04:21.709 --> 00:04:23.720
as well as cloud services.

00:04:23.720 --> 00:04:25.082
Is that accurate?

00:04:25.082 --> 00:04:25.790
PAUL TURNER: Yes.

00:04:25.790 --> 00:04:27.860
There's three kind
of major variants

00:04:27.860 --> 00:04:29.660
that were discovered
of this attack.

00:04:29.660 --> 00:04:33.410
Each of them apply
to various sub cases

00:04:33.410 --> 00:04:35.960
of how we use computers today.

00:04:35.960 --> 00:04:39.230
Variant 1 is
particularly important

00:04:39.230 --> 00:04:42.770
relative to the encapsulation
that we use for things

00:04:42.770 --> 00:04:44.290
like JavaScript engines.

00:04:44.290 --> 00:04:47.060
Variant 2 is particularly
important with respect

00:04:47.060 --> 00:04:49.910
to how we isolate
virtualized machines.

00:04:49.910 --> 00:04:51.530
You can think of cloud hosting.

00:04:51.530 --> 00:04:54.440
And Variant 3 is also
really important,

00:04:54.440 --> 00:04:58.730
in that it's the most direct
way on a consumer device

00:04:58.730 --> 00:05:03.510
to steal some of these data
if you are able to arrange

00:05:03.510 --> 00:05:05.060
for local execution.

00:05:05.060 --> 00:05:07.185
MARK MANDEL: So that's
actually really interesting.

00:05:07.185 --> 00:05:09.980
Because I've noticed,
as a consumer,

00:05:09.980 --> 00:05:11.880
I've got information
to, like, patch stuff

00:05:11.880 --> 00:05:14.047
on my cloud provider, as
well as upgrade my browser.

00:05:14.047 --> 00:05:15.713
And I think I've seen
stuff about, like,

00:05:15.713 --> 00:05:17.280
upgrading the driver on my GPU.

00:05:17.280 --> 00:05:20.040
Is that because there are
just a variety of ways

00:05:20.040 --> 00:05:22.170
of taking advantage
of this vulnerability?

00:05:22.170 --> 00:05:24.510
PAUL TURNER: Exactly
When you see suggestions

00:05:24.510 --> 00:05:26.370
that you should patch
your operating system,

00:05:26.370 --> 00:05:28.740
many of those really are
looking at the third variant

00:05:28.740 --> 00:05:30.930
where you can attack
local system memory.

00:05:30.930 --> 00:05:33.250
Whereas, some of these
other variants that we see,

00:05:33.250 --> 00:05:35.900
Variant 1, Variant 2-- commonly
referred to in the media

00:05:35.900 --> 00:05:37.500
as Spectre--

00:05:37.500 --> 00:05:40.357
they are seeing providers
applying the protections.

00:05:40.357 --> 00:05:42.690
You're seeing browsers adding
protections for Variant 1.

00:05:42.690 --> 00:05:44.870
You're seeing cloud
providers having protections

00:05:44.870 --> 00:05:45.830
for Variant 1 and 2.

00:05:45.830 --> 00:05:48.780
But like always in
security, protection

00:05:48.780 --> 00:05:50.740
means fixing everything.

00:05:50.740 --> 00:05:52.530
And some of those
things are intended

00:05:52.530 --> 00:05:53.940
to be employed by consumers.

00:05:53.940 --> 00:05:56.356
MELANIE WARRICK: And actually,
when you mentioned Spectre,

00:05:56.356 --> 00:05:57.495
I wanted to ask you--

00:05:57.495 --> 00:06:00.489
we have heard of the terms
Meltdown and Spectre.

00:06:00.489 --> 00:06:02.280
But I also know the
blog post that you guys

00:06:02.280 --> 00:06:05.040
provided doesn't necessarily
mention those explicitly.

00:06:05.040 --> 00:06:07.254
Matt, you wanted to mention
a little bit about that?

00:06:07.254 --> 00:06:08.670
MATT LINTON: Yeah,
so there's been

00:06:08.670 --> 00:06:11.550
a decent amount of confusion
out there over Spectre,

00:06:11.550 --> 00:06:13.500
and Meltdown and all
of the other variants.

00:06:13.500 --> 00:06:15.750
The background behind
this is that there

00:06:15.750 --> 00:06:17.250
was a little bit
of a co-discovery

00:06:17.250 --> 00:06:19.330
going on but this vulnerability.

00:06:19.330 --> 00:06:21.180
Jann Horn from
Google's Project Zero

00:06:21.180 --> 00:06:23.490
discovered the
vulnerability and kind

00:06:23.490 --> 00:06:26.640
of teased it out into
three distinct variants

00:06:26.640 --> 00:06:28.650
that we call
Variant 1, 2, and 3.

00:06:28.650 --> 00:06:30.930
A few months later,
another research team,

00:06:30.930 --> 00:06:35.340
who was also looking at the
same sort of way to trick CPUs,

00:06:35.340 --> 00:06:37.920
discovered the
vulnerabilities, as well.

00:06:37.920 --> 00:06:39.960
And they chose to
give them names.

00:06:39.960 --> 00:06:41.580
Naming a vulnerability
is something

00:06:41.580 --> 00:06:44.220
in the security industry
that is kind of a little bit

00:06:44.220 --> 00:06:44.922
preferential.

00:06:44.922 --> 00:06:47.005
If you like to name and
logo your vulnerabilities,

00:06:47.005 --> 00:06:47.760
you'll do it.

00:06:47.760 --> 00:06:51.180
And if you find it to be
uncouth, you don't do it.

00:06:51.180 --> 00:06:53.472
So Project Zero did not
choose to give names

00:06:53.472 --> 00:06:54.430
to the vulnerabilities.

00:06:54.430 --> 00:06:56.350
But the other researchers did.

00:06:56.350 --> 00:07:00.365
And so they named them
Spectre for Variants 1 and 2

00:07:00.365 --> 00:07:02.370
and Meltdown for Variant 3.

00:07:02.370 --> 00:07:04.290
And the reason we ended
up with two names that

00:07:04.290 --> 00:07:07.110
covers three vulnerabilities
is that the team that

00:07:07.110 --> 00:07:10.080
named Spectre hadn't
discovered all

00:07:10.080 --> 00:07:12.360
of the things that
make Variants 1

00:07:12.360 --> 00:07:14.029
and 2 unique from one another.

00:07:14.029 --> 00:07:15.820
And so they thought
that they had two bugs,

00:07:15.820 --> 00:07:17.100
but they really had three.

00:07:17.100 --> 00:07:19.290
So we ended up out here
with this naming confusion

00:07:19.290 --> 00:07:23.850
where Spectre covers Variant
1, which is most [INAUDIBLE]

00:07:23.850 --> 00:07:26.450
in gits and
browsers, and things.

00:07:26.450 --> 00:07:28.650
Variant 2, which is
the most exploitable,

00:07:28.650 --> 00:07:30.420
as a hypervisor bypass--

00:07:30.420 --> 00:07:32.130
and then the whole
separate Variant 3

00:07:32.130 --> 00:07:35.550
that they call Meltdown, which
is most exploitable, in terms

00:07:35.550 --> 00:07:37.865
of interacting with the
operating system itself.

00:07:37.865 --> 00:07:39.240
PAUL TURNER: And
the thing that's

00:07:39.240 --> 00:07:43.140
important to know there is where
Matt said Variant 1 and 2 apply

00:07:43.140 --> 00:07:46.980
to the browser and
hypervisor and Variant 3

00:07:46.980 --> 00:07:49.590
applies to the host
operating system,

00:07:49.590 --> 00:07:53.610
for all consumers out there, the
patches for Linux and Windows

00:07:53.610 --> 00:07:58.240
are already being included
in those operating systems.

00:07:58.240 --> 00:08:02.216
So as long as they upgrade
to use the latest versions,

00:08:02.216 --> 00:08:03.090
they will be covered.

00:08:03.090 --> 00:08:05.280
MELANIE WARRICK: And that means
MacBooks, as well as those who

00:08:05.280 --> 00:08:06.694
use the Linux operating system.

00:08:06.694 --> 00:08:07.860
PAUL TURNER: That's correct.

00:08:07.860 --> 00:08:09.930
Apple has also published
patches for this one early.

00:08:09.930 --> 00:08:11.560
I should have included
in the previous list.

00:08:11.560 --> 00:08:12.420
MELANIE WARRICK: No worries.

00:08:12.420 --> 00:08:13.610
MARK MANDEL: So this
is interesting, too.

00:08:13.610 --> 00:08:15.270
So you said that
the vulnerability

00:08:15.270 --> 00:08:16.527
is at a CPU level.

00:08:16.527 --> 00:08:19.110
But a lot of these patches are
coming out what are essentially

00:08:19.110 --> 00:08:20.190
a software level.

00:08:20.190 --> 00:08:23.040
How do these fixes work then?

00:08:23.040 --> 00:08:24.984
How does that help people?

00:08:24.984 --> 00:08:26.400
PAUL TURNER: The
fundamental thing

00:08:26.400 --> 00:08:30.600
that all the fixes are trying to
do is arrange for the execution

00:08:30.600 --> 00:08:34.620
to be occurring in a way
so that the tricks that are

00:08:34.620 --> 00:08:37.230
being played no longer apply.

00:08:37.230 --> 00:08:40.020
In the case of
Meltdown, for example,

00:08:40.020 --> 00:08:43.020
you are tricking the
CPU into looking up

00:08:43.020 --> 00:08:46.800
memory that it shouldn't
normally be able to access.

00:08:46.800 --> 00:08:48.870
And the way that
that was mitigated

00:08:48.870 --> 00:08:52.850
was by, instead of using the
regular access protections

00:08:52.850 --> 00:08:56.430
on that memory, completely
unmapping it in those contexts

00:08:56.430 --> 00:08:58.200
that it should not be accessed.

00:08:58.200 --> 00:09:01.290
This meant that, even if
speculative execution were

00:09:01.290 --> 00:09:04.890
to try to access it, it
didn't actually know how.

00:09:04.890 --> 00:09:08.624
So that's where Variant 1 and 2
have similar kind of approaches

00:09:08.624 --> 00:09:11.040
where we're trying to find
ways to restructure the program

00:09:11.040 --> 00:09:15.540
control flow so that these
ways that you can influence

00:09:15.540 --> 00:09:19.760
or bias when the CPU is
guessing what to do next

00:09:19.760 --> 00:09:21.122
are constrained or eliminated.

00:09:21.122 --> 00:09:23.330
MATT LINTON: I think there's
a good opportunity here,

00:09:23.330 --> 00:09:27.300
too, to talk about how the
words fix and mitigate tend

00:09:27.300 --> 00:09:29.790
to be used very intentionally
by security engineers

00:09:29.790 --> 00:09:32.550
to mean specific things that
other people might not really

00:09:32.550 --> 00:09:33.570
put together.

00:09:33.570 --> 00:09:35.910
When we say that a
vulnerability is fixed,

00:09:35.910 --> 00:09:38.910
we mean that the actual root
vulnerability is no longer

00:09:38.910 --> 00:09:40.630
present in that device.

00:09:40.630 --> 00:09:43.770
A lot of the
software mitigations

00:09:43.770 --> 00:09:45.390
that you're seeing
come out, these

00:09:45.390 --> 00:09:47.880
patches for Windows
and things, they're not

00:09:47.880 --> 00:09:49.404
fixing the
vulnerability, so much

00:09:49.404 --> 00:09:50.820
as they're mitigating
it by making

00:09:50.820 --> 00:09:54.069
it impossible for someone
to take advantage of.

00:09:54.069 --> 00:09:55.860
And the analogy that
I like to use for that

00:09:55.860 --> 00:09:58.140
is, if you have a
broken window, you

00:09:58.140 --> 00:10:00.900
can fix the window by
replacing the window glass.

00:10:00.900 --> 00:10:02.990
You can also put a board
over it to mitigate

00:10:02.990 --> 00:10:04.340
the hole in the house.

00:10:04.340 --> 00:10:06.090
You haven't actually
fixed the window yet.

00:10:06.090 --> 00:10:08.776
You've just made the hole in
the house impossible for someone

00:10:08.776 --> 00:10:09.985
to come in through.

00:10:09.985 --> 00:10:12.610
MELANIE WARRICK: So, in terms of
the fixes and the mitigations,

00:10:12.610 --> 00:10:15.810
especially from a standpoint
of the actual processors,

00:10:15.810 --> 00:10:20.730
I know at CES, the CEO
of Intel, Brian Krzanich,

00:10:20.730 --> 00:10:25.500
had mentioned that they have
basically provided a patch

00:10:25.500 --> 00:10:27.870
for 90% of the vulnerabilities
affecting the processes--

00:10:27.870 --> 00:10:29.670
or they will be by
the end of the week--

00:10:29.670 --> 00:10:32.850
and that they are expecting
to handle the remaining 10

00:10:32.850 --> 00:10:34.530
by the end of
January without them

00:10:34.530 --> 00:10:37.252
requiring to recall any chips.

00:10:37.252 --> 00:10:38.460
Did you guys hear about that?

00:10:38.460 --> 00:10:40.200
Did you have any
additional comments

00:10:40.200 --> 00:10:42.030
that you wanted to
make on that one?

00:10:42.030 --> 00:10:44.630
PAUL TURNER: I would extend the
analogy that Matt just made.

00:10:44.630 --> 00:10:47.130
It's clear that, for the
current generation of CPUs,

00:10:47.130 --> 00:10:50.070
we will only be able to develop
mitigations and not fixes.

00:10:50.070 --> 00:10:52.110
So we're going to be
driving with the boards

00:10:52.110 --> 00:10:53.880
on the windows for a long time.

00:10:53.880 --> 00:10:56.400
We do expect that, in
future generations,

00:10:56.400 --> 00:11:00.100
that these problems will be
fixed at the hardware level.

00:11:00.100 --> 00:11:04.830
However, we are also optimistic
that, for all current CPUs,

00:11:04.830 --> 00:11:07.350
the mitigations can be
deployed so that we do not

00:11:07.350 --> 00:11:09.900
need to replace or
update the hardware.

00:11:09.900 --> 00:11:11.858
MELANIE WARRICK: So what
are some of the things

00:11:11.858 --> 00:11:13.650
we've done for GCP,
and G Suite, and also

00:11:13.650 --> 00:11:16.920
additional internal Google
products and services that

00:11:16.920 --> 00:11:17.880
are provided?

00:11:17.880 --> 00:11:20.280
PAUL TURNER: There, we've
deployed-- and in many cases,

00:11:20.280 --> 00:11:21.840
developed-- the
mitigations that are

00:11:21.840 --> 00:11:24.575
being used across the industry.

00:11:24.575 --> 00:11:26.700
We have to talk about them
on a case-by-case basis.

00:11:26.700 --> 00:11:28.074
Because there are
three variants.

00:11:28.074 --> 00:11:30.190
And they each have their
own mitigations or fixes.

00:11:30.190 --> 00:11:34.740
However, in all cases, we have
deployed fixes and mitigations

00:11:34.740 --> 00:11:37.770
that we believe
cover all the known

00:11:37.770 --> 00:11:40.170
classes of the current
vulnerabilities

00:11:40.170 --> 00:11:42.692
and ensure that consumer
data remains protected.

00:11:42.692 --> 00:11:44.400
MATT LINTON: Yeah,
it's difficult to talk

00:11:44.400 --> 00:11:46.950
about this succinctly
in Cloud, especially.

00:11:46.950 --> 00:11:49.410
Because this vulnerability
affects so many things.

00:11:49.410 --> 00:11:53.730
And Cloud, by nature, affects
people at different levels.

00:11:53.730 --> 00:11:55.590
You have software
as a service where

00:11:55.590 --> 00:11:58.830
somebody is buying the software
and access to it from us.

00:11:58.830 --> 00:12:03.810
Like Drives, Docs, G-mail,
we're providing everything

00:12:03.810 --> 00:12:05.500
from the software on down.

00:12:05.500 --> 00:12:07.890
And it's very easy for
us to easily just say,

00:12:07.890 --> 00:12:09.570
having mitigated our
infrastructure that

00:12:09.570 --> 00:12:11.400
runs those products,
and having checked

00:12:11.400 --> 00:12:13.170
the software for
their abilities,

00:12:13.170 --> 00:12:15.420
we're confident we
protected people there.

00:12:15.420 --> 00:12:17.070
But then there's
things like GCE, which

00:12:17.070 --> 00:12:18.960
is infrastructure as a service.

00:12:18.960 --> 00:12:21.570
People are paying
us to run everything

00:12:21.570 --> 00:12:23.970
from the hardware
for them, and manage

00:12:23.970 --> 00:12:26.740
scalability, and network,
and all those things.

00:12:26.740 --> 00:12:28.680
But they also want to
run their own operating

00:12:28.680 --> 00:12:30.370
system inside a container.

00:12:30.370 --> 00:12:32.370
It's more complicated
there, because we've

00:12:32.370 --> 00:12:34.620
protected all of
the things that they

00:12:34.620 --> 00:12:36.930
rely on us to protect for them.

00:12:36.930 --> 00:12:39.270
But you can't simply
say, everything's fixed.

00:12:39.270 --> 00:12:42.360
Because if a customer runs
their own operating system,

00:12:42.360 --> 00:12:44.790
and they're worried
about attacks occurring

00:12:44.790 --> 00:12:47.592
from within their OS to
their own data stored in it,

00:12:47.592 --> 00:12:49.050
they also have a
little bit of work

00:12:49.050 --> 00:12:50.640
to do patching for
these variants.

00:12:50.640 --> 00:12:51.610
As well.

00:12:51.610 --> 00:12:53.068
PAUL TURNER: A good
example of this

00:12:53.068 --> 00:12:55.900
would be a multi-tenant customer
where someone might be doing

00:12:55.900 --> 00:13:00.240
re-hosting where they're
hosting from a cloud provider,

00:13:00.240 --> 00:13:02.820
such as Google, or
Amazon, or Microsoft.

00:13:02.820 --> 00:13:06.240
And then they're internally
kind of releasing that space

00:13:06.240 --> 00:13:07.765
to their own uses.

00:13:07.765 --> 00:13:11.400
There, the attacks that Matt
just described, in many ways,

00:13:11.400 --> 00:13:13.980
they are hosting
provider themselves.

00:13:13.980 --> 00:13:17.730
And so the infrastructure
that they've contributed that

00:13:17.730 --> 00:13:20.580
runs on top of the underlying
[INAUDIBLE] providers,

00:13:20.580 --> 00:13:23.315
such as Google,
Microsoft, Amazon,

00:13:23.315 --> 00:13:25.620
that infrastructure
must also be protected.

00:13:25.620 --> 00:13:27.150
And that's something
that they must

00:13:27.150 --> 00:13:29.107
do in their own
development and plan.

00:13:29.107 --> 00:13:30.940
MARK MANDEL: I want to
go back a little bit.

00:13:30.940 --> 00:13:31.980
We've been talking sort
of about mitigation,

00:13:31.980 --> 00:13:34.700
and fixes-- and that's a really
interesting turn of phrase.

00:13:34.700 --> 00:13:36.570
And maybe to extend
the metaphor,

00:13:36.570 --> 00:13:42.060
if I have a boarded-up window,
it's nowhere near as pretty as,

00:13:42.060 --> 00:13:42.990
say, a fixed window.

00:13:42.990 --> 00:13:44.730
But it sort of works
kind of the same.

00:13:44.730 --> 00:13:47.100
But maybe I don't
have as nice a house.

00:13:47.100 --> 00:13:49.290
If we're talking
about mitigations

00:13:49.290 --> 00:13:51.210
for this vulnerability,
are there any sort

00:13:51.210 --> 00:13:54.330
of side effects or consequences
of these mitigations that

00:13:54.330 --> 00:13:57.450
may end up sort of in the
wild that may be necessary,

00:13:57.450 --> 00:14:00.129
but should be stuff that people
should look out for maybe?

00:14:00.129 --> 00:14:01.920
PAUL TURNER: That's a
really good question.

00:14:01.920 --> 00:14:04.225
Obviously, as you say,
a boarded-up window

00:14:04.225 --> 00:14:06.600
is not something that's going
to add value to a property.

00:14:06.600 --> 00:14:08.550
So we've been very
careful in how

00:14:08.550 --> 00:14:11.520
we develop these mitigations
to try and make sure

00:14:11.520 --> 00:14:14.920
that the effects of that
were not observable.

00:14:14.920 --> 00:14:17.100
It's definitely true that,
in early versions when

00:14:17.100 --> 00:14:19.610
we were kind of prototyping,
developing internally,

00:14:19.610 --> 00:14:24.570
we were seeing overhead for many
of these fixes or mitigations.

00:14:24.570 --> 00:14:29.160
But as we evolved and
found newer and better ways

00:14:29.160 --> 00:14:31.530
to add those
protections, we really

00:14:31.530 --> 00:14:34.200
were able to minimize
the overhead that we saw.

00:14:34.200 --> 00:14:37.470
In many cases, we actually
see no perceptible overheads

00:14:37.470 --> 00:14:39.390
for both our own
and custom workloads

00:14:39.390 --> 00:14:41.160
while still providing
these protections.

00:14:41.160 --> 00:14:42.951
MATT LINTON: Yeah, we
boarded up the window

00:14:42.951 --> 00:14:44.610
with clear plexiglass
that's mostly

00:14:44.610 --> 00:14:46.380
indistinguishable
from the old window.

00:14:46.380 --> 00:14:48.060
MELANIE WARRICK:
And in terms of GCP,

00:14:48.060 --> 00:14:51.300
in regards to what was being
updated and boarded up,

00:14:51.300 --> 00:14:53.055
was there any
maintenance downtime?

00:14:53.055 --> 00:14:54.180
PAUL TURNER: There was not.

00:14:54.180 --> 00:14:55.555
MELANIE WARRICK:
You've described

00:14:55.555 --> 00:14:57.450
the guest and host-level
mitigations that

00:14:57.450 --> 00:14:59.190
have been taking place.

00:14:59.190 --> 00:15:00.660
What do you need
to do in regards

00:15:00.660 --> 00:15:04.080
to achieving those
host-level mitigations being

00:15:04.080 --> 00:15:05.615
applied to your GCP instances?

00:15:05.615 --> 00:15:06.990
PAUL TURNER: So
when you say host

00:15:06.990 --> 00:15:08.690
here, what you're
really talking about

00:15:08.690 --> 00:15:11.070
is the Google level
mitigations right?

00:15:11.070 --> 00:15:13.584
When we say a host, we
mean the infrastructure

00:15:13.584 --> 00:15:15.500
that's running the virtual
machines that we're

00:15:15.500 --> 00:15:16.960
providing to our users.

00:15:16.960 --> 00:15:18.830
There, we're actually
very fortunate,

00:15:18.830 --> 00:15:22.550
in that we have technology we
called live migration, which

00:15:22.550 --> 00:15:26.990
allows us to move a running
guest between host instances

00:15:26.990 --> 00:15:29.630
without requiring a reboot
or otherwise interfering

00:15:29.630 --> 00:15:30.650
with the guest.

00:15:30.650 --> 00:15:33.530
We were able to actually use
this to transparently deploy

00:15:33.530 --> 00:15:36.560
all of our Variant 1 and
Variant 2 mitigations

00:15:36.560 --> 00:15:41.099
for our infrastructure with
no customer-reported downtime.

00:15:41.099 --> 00:15:42.890
MARK MANDEL: I just
want to hit this point.

00:15:42.890 --> 00:15:44.977
People do need to upgrade
their own operating

00:15:44.977 --> 00:15:46.560
systems if they're
managing themselves

00:15:46.560 --> 00:15:49.600
on something like GCE, or
GKE, or something like that.

00:15:49.600 --> 00:15:50.600
PAUL TURNER: Definitely.

00:15:50.600 --> 00:15:52.370
As a matter of best
practice, users

00:15:52.370 --> 00:15:54.420
should be patching their
own operating system.

00:15:54.420 --> 00:15:56.290
The vulnerability
that's most important

00:15:56.290 --> 00:15:59.570
there is Variant 3,
as known as Meltdown.

00:15:59.570 --> 00:16:02.390
While that attack will
not allow information

00:16:02.390 --> 00:16:05.960
to escape between the
hypervisor or our infrastructure

00:16:05.960 --> 00:16:08.210
and their operating
system, it is possible

00:16:08.210 --> 00:16:11.600
that local attacks from within
their VMs on their own data

00:16:11.600 --> 00:16:14.030
may succeed without
those patches.

00:16:14.030 --> 00:16:15.890
This is really a matter
of best practice.

00:16:15.890 --> 00:16:17.720
MELANIE WARRICK: Something I
keep hearing from people is,

00:16:17.720 --> 00:16:19.350
make sure you update
your software.

00:16:19.350 --> 00:16:21.590
Whether it's OS, whether
you're on a phone,

00:16:21.590 --> 00:16:24.292
whether you're on your
operating system on your laptop,

00:16:24.292 --> 00:16:26.750
you always want to make sure,
when you see new patches come

00:16:26.750 --> 00:16:28.212
through, to keep updating them.

00:16:28.212 --> 00:16:30.170
Do you guys have any
additional recommendations

00:16:30.170 --> 00:16:32.000
for best practices
around security?

00:16:32.000 --> 00:16:32.750
MATT LINTON: Yeah.

00:16:32.750 --> 00:16:35.630
I would say that, anytime
a complex vulnerability

00:16:35.630 --> 00:16:38.030
gets discussed, a lot of
people turn to wondering, well,

00:16:38.030 --> 00:16:39.230
what should I do about it?

00:16:39.230 --> 00:16:43.850
And in security, we kind of
have consistent messaging out

00:16:43.850 --> 00:16:46.460
that, most of the
time, the answer

00:16:46.460 --> 00:16:48.960
is, keep yourself
updated with software

00:16:48.960 --> 00:16:51.230
patches from the
vendor, and make sure

00:16:51.230 --> 00:16:53.240
that you're using
two-factor authentication,

00:16:53.240 --> 00:16:57.164
and other protective layers on
anything you care deeply about.

00:16:57.164 --> 00:16:58.580
And the reason we
always come back

00:16:58.580 --> 00:17:01.880
to hammering on that
is, most of the time,

00:17:01.880 --> 00:17:03.524
those are the two
most effective things

00:17:03.524 --> 00:17:04.940
you can do to
protect yourself not

00:17:04.940 --> 00:17:07.640
just against this
vulnerability, but in general

00:17:07.640 --> 00:17:10.030
against vulnerabilities
that you know about,

00:17:10.030 --> 00:17:11.869
vulnerabilities that
you don't know about,

00:17:11.869 --> 00:17:14.660
tomorrow's big discovery
that may come out.

00:17:14.660 --> 00:17:18.234
It's all going to end up boiling
down to take care of the things

00:17:18.234 --> 00:17:18.859
you're running.

00:17:18.859 --> 00:17:19.819
Keep them updated.

00:17:19.819 --> 00:17:21.800
And keep your
authentication safe.

00:17:21.800 --> 00:17:24.109
PAUL TURNER: It really does
come down to best practice.

00:17:24.109 --> 00:17:26.020
The guidelines that
we're proposing here

00:17:26.020 --> 00:17:29.480
are uniform within the security
industry, with the idea,

00:17:29.480 --> 00:17:31.640
as Matt said, that
they leave you

00:17:31.640 --> 00:17:35.000
in the best position for
unknown vulnerabilities

00:17:35.000 --> 00:17:36.500
that you may not know about.

00:17:36.500 --> 00:17:39.230
And because, often, when
a vulnerability like

00:17:39.230 --> 00:17:41.180
this is discovered,
you have to be

00:17:41.180 --> 00:17:43.350
able to update your
software quickly.

00:17:43.350 --> 00:17:45.680
And if you have not
been keeping up-to-date

00:17:45.680 --> 00:17:48.020
with other patches or
security improvements

00:17:48.020 --> 00:17:51.590
in the infrastructure, you could
have a large technical debt,

00:17:51.590 --> 00:17:54.350
which you might have to mitigate
in a very quick fashion when

00:17:54.350 --> 00:17:55.650
something like this comes out.

00:17:55.650 --> 00:17:59.420
So those practices
are both preventative

00:17:59.420 --> 00:18:00.890
and in making sure
that you can be

00:18:00.890 --> 00:18:03.530
as agile as possible in
responding to these issues

00:18:03.530 --> 00:18:04.197
when they arise.

00:18:04.197 --> 00:18:05.571
MARK MANDEL: So
you're not saying

00:18:05.571 --> 00:18:07.490
that there should be
any change in the way

00:18:07.490 --> 00:18:09.900
that people are handling
their security best practices.

00:18:09.900 --> 00:18:12.530
Really, the
tried-and-true methods

00:18:12.530 --> 00:18:13.982
stay the same way they are.

00:18:13.982 --> 00:18:14.690
PAUL TURNER: Yes.

00:18:14.690 --> 00:18:17.750
Generally speaking,
Variant 3 or Meltdown

00:18:17.750 --> 00:18:19.560
is not externally exploitable.

00:18:19.560 --> 00:18:21.834
So users are only
really vulnerable

00:18:21.834 --> 00:18:23.750
from their own software,
because we've already

00:18:23.750 --> 00:18:26.840
patched our exposure to Variant
1 and 2 and their exposure

00:18:26.840 --> 00:18:29.210
to Variant 1 and 2 from
our infrastructure.

00:18:29.210 --> 00:18:33.240
But again, we really do believe
this is the best practice.

00:18:33.240 --> 00:18:36.429
We strongly encourage all of
our users to apply these fixes,

00:18:36.429 --> 00:18:38.470
just as they would any
other security [INAUDIBLE]

00:18:38.470 --> 00:18:42.550
fix to ensure that,
as new ways to attack

00:18:42.550 --> 00:18:45.470
both this and other parts of
the system are discovered,

00:18:45.470 --> 00:18:47.030
they can continue
to be protected

00:18:47.030 --> 00:18:48.207
as quickly as possible.

00:18:48.207 --> 00:18:50.540
MELANIE WARRICK: Can you guys
comment a little bit, too,

00:18:50.540 --> 00:18:51.300
on the fact--

00:18:51.300 --> 00:18:52.910
I know, in the blog
post and with what

00:18:52.910 --> 00:18:56.600
Project Zero released, they were
saying how this is identified,

00:18:56.600 --> 00:18:58.220
and then there was
coordinated efforts

00:18:58.220 --> 00:19:00.890
to try to do the
fixing and the patching

00:19:00.890 --> 00:19:02.934
before it was going to
be announced publicly.

00:19:02.934 --> 00:19:04.850
Would you be able to
come in a little bit more

00:19:04.850 --> 00:19:07.349
about that process and what
that looks like for coordination

00:19:07.349 --> 00:19:08.327
with security?

00:19:08.327 --> 00:19:09.410
MATT LINTON: To an extent.

00:19:09.410 --> 00:19:12.140
This is one of those things
that is really difficult

00:19:12.140 --> 00:19:14.720
and always gets handled on
a case-by-case basis, which

00:19:14.720 --> 00:19:16.790
is, you have a giant bug.

00:19:16.790 --> 00:19:19.220
You have a lot of work
you need to do to fix it.

00:19:19.220 --> 00:19:21.650
Other people you know
also have a lot of work

00:19:21.650 --> 00:19:23.810
that need to happen to fix it.

00:19:23.810 --> 00:19:26.420
And then you end up
with inter-dependencies.

00:19:26.420 --> 00:19:29.480
If Google were to fix
this bug all on our own

00:19:29.480 --> 00:19:32.810
without contacting anyone else,
than other operating system

00:19:32.810 --> 00:19:34.490
vendors, like Apple
and Microsoft,

00:19:34.490 --> 00:19:35.750
wouldn't be protected.

00:19:35.750 --> 00:19:38.220
And our users would
be left at risk.

00:19:38.220 --> 00:19:40.580
So for something of
this scale, there's

00:19:40.580 --> 00:19:43.100
always decisions that need
to be made around, what

00:19:43.100 --> 00:19:45.990
other companies need to know?

00:19:45.990 --> 00:19:47.810
They need to know to
protect the users.

00:19:47.810 --> 00:19:51.060
Do they need to know so
we can protect our users,

00:19:51.060 --> 00:19:52.790
so we can protect ourselves?

00:19:52.790 --> 00:19:55.700
And sort of an ad
hoc group ends up

00:19:55.700 --> 00:19:57.770
forming of people
who are affected, who

00:19:57.770 --> 00:20:00.650
believe they have something
to contribute to fix it,

00:20:00.650 --> 00:20:04.250
and who are all sort
of giving and receiving

00:20:04.250 --> 00:20:07.460
advice and technical
data from one another

00:20:07.460 --> 00:20:09.770
to try to put all the
users on the internet in as

00:20:09.770 --> 00:20:12.980
good a footing as possible
when we're ready to talk

00:20:12.980 --> 00:20:16.070
about the issue without
creating such a large group

00:20:16.070 --> 00:20:18.706
that it becomes impossible
to keep the issue contained.

00:20:18.706 --> 00:20:21.080
PAUL TURNER: And this really
was an industry-wide effort.

00:20:21.080 --> 00:20:25.250
Our own attempts at mitigation
began at the same time

00:20:25.250 --> 00:20:26.510
as other industry players.

00:20:26.510 --> 00:20:29.090
We weren't trying to provide
a head start for ourselves

00:20:29.090 --> 00:20:30.720
or any other advantage.

00:20:30.720 --> 00:20:33.740
We really saw this as
an industry-wide problem

00:20:33.740 --> 00:20:36.880
that everybody would have
to try to contribute,

00:20:36.880 --> 00:20:39.350
both in terms of
finding ways to mitigate

00:20:39.350 --> 00:20:42.687
these complicated and
difficult vulnerabilities,

00:20:42.687 --> 00:20:44.270
and in making sure
that everyone would

00:20:44.270 --> 00:20:46.240
have the chance to
deploy protection

00:20:46.240 --> 00:20:47.680
before our disclosure occured.

00:20:47.680 --> 00:20:49.346
MATT LINTON: Yeah,
there is a little bit

00:20:49.346 --> 00:20:51.890
of a thing called supply chain,
which plays into this a lot,

00:20:51.890 --> 00:20:52.650
too.

00:20:52.650 --> 00:20:55.400
And supply chain is a
security industry term

00:20:55.400 --> 00:20:58.970
which references the fact that
you inherit vulnerabilities

00:20:58.970 --> 00:21:01.860
from those you trust to
provide you with things.

00:21:01.860 --> 00:21:06.140
And so I'm speaking to you
right now from a MacBook Pro.

00:21:06.140 --> 00:21:08.150
I have a supply chain
dependency on Apple

00:21:08.150 --> 00:21:11.120
to make sure Apple is
secure if I am to do my job

00:21:11.120 --> 00:21:12.800
and keep Google secure.

00:21:12.800 --> 00:21:17.000
So in a lot of ways, these sort
of multi-industry partnerships

00:21:17.000 --> 00:21:20.630
for something this size are
brought about by necessity

00:21:20.630 --> 00:21:22.910
from the fact that the
supply chain forces us all

00:21:22.910 --> 00:21:26.335
to adopt at least a little
bit of each other's problems.

00:21:26.335 --> 00:21:28.460
MARK MANDEL: You kind of
said something interesting

00:21:28.460 --> 00:21:30.543
that I don't know if people
who aren't necessarily

00:21:30.543 --> 00:21:32.900
familiar with how security
research sort of goes.

00:21:32.900 --> 00:21:34.642
You mentioned that,
up until the point--

00:21:34.642 --> 00:21:37.100
I think you said-- that it was
going to be public or it was

00:21:37.100 --> 00:21:39.330
going to be shown to
the outside world--

00:21:39.330 --> 00:21:42.080
can you talk a little about
how that research process goes,

00:21:42.080 --> 00:21:44.540
about finding vulnerabilities,
and then finally making them

00:21:44.540 --> 00:21:46.970
public, and the steps that
sort of go through that,

00:21:46.970 --> 00:21:48.110
and why those steps exist?

00:21:48.110 --> 00:21:48.860
MATT LINTON: Sure.

00:21:48.860 --> 00:21:51.740
Those steps exist as
sort of a compromise

00:21:51.740 --> 00:21:55.230
that has slowly developed over
time in the IT industry itself.

00:21:55.230 --> 00:21:57.440
There are processes
often, these days,

00:21:57.440 --> 00:21:59.720
called coordinated disclosure.

00:21:59.720 --> 00:22:01.700
And what happens is
a security researcher

00:22:01.700 --> 00:22:03.380
finds a vulnerability.

00:22:03.380 --> 00:22:05.630
And they report it
to whoever it is

00:22:05.630 --> 00:22:08.510
that's actually makes the
product the vulnerability is

00:22:08.510 --> 00:22:09.440
in.

00:22:09.440 --> 00:22:12.230
And then the vendor
and the researcher

00:22:12.230 --> 00:22:15.320
agree upon a period of time in
which the vendor gets a head

00:22:15.320 --> 00:22:17.720
start on fixing it to
actually protect people

00:22:17.720 --> 00:22:19.970
before the researcher
gets to publish it.

00:22:19.970 --> 00:22:23.990
That's in contrast to a behavior
called full disclosure, which

00:22:23.990 --> 00:22:25.880
is when researchers
find a vulnerability

00:22:25.880 --> 00:22:27.620
and immediately disclose
it to the world.

00:22:27.620 --> 00:22:29.299
There's a lot of pros and cons.

00:22:29.299 --> 00:22:31.340
And I don't think, probably,
this is a good place

00:22:31.340 --> 00:22:34.090
to go into it, unless you
have a couple extra hours.

00:22:34.090 --> 00:22:36.470
But I can tell you,
what Project Zero does

00:22:36.470 --> 00:22:38.630
is they have a
standard process where

00:22:38.630 --> 00:22:40.220
they find a vulnerability.

00:22:40.220 --> 00:22:42.899
They notify the maintainer
in whose product

00:22:42.899 --> 00:22:43.940
the vulnerability exists.

00:22:43.940 --> 00:22:47.210
And they give them 90
days to remediate it

00:22:47.210 --> 00:22:49.850
before Project
Zero discloses it.

00:22:49.850 --> 00:22:53.330
And if they vendor is able to
remediate it earlier, then they

00:22:53.330 --> 00:22:56.300
disclose it earlier in
coordination with the vendor.

00:22:56.300 --> 00:22:58.760
And that's sort of
a compromise that

00:22:58.760 --> 00:23:03.080
has been reached between the
pros and cons that hit you

00:23:03.080 --> 00:23:06.320
when you go full disclosure and
the pros and cons that hit you

00:23:06.320 --> 00:23:09.350
when you allow the vendor to
completely control disclosure.

00:23:09.350 --> 00:23:11.750
So much of the security
research industry

00:23:11.750 --> 00:23:13.670
right now lives in
that compromised zone

00:23:13.670 --> 00:23:17.229
of coordinated disclosure
with a defined time period.

00:23:17.229 --> 00:23:19.520
MELANIE WARRICK: And this
got released a little earlier

00:23:19.520 --> 00:23:20.300
than expected.

00:23:20.300 --> 00:23:21.200
Is that right?

00:23:21.200 --> 00:23:23.330
PAUL TURNER: Yeah, relative
to what I was saying,

00:23:23.330 --> 00:23:26.060
I think the actual
more interesting point

00:23:26.060 --> 00:23:30.830
there is this was an example of
a issue that was so pervasive

00:23:30.830 --> 00:23:35.420
and affected so many users
that we actually extended

00:23:35.420 --> 00:23:37.130
that standard 90-day policy.

00:23:37.130 --> 00:23:40.530
Because it was clear that
all of the supply chain level

00:23:40.530 --> 00:23:43.040
mitigation that
users would require

00:23:43.040 --> 00:23:46.240
would not be possible to
deploy within that window.

00:23:46.240 --> 00:23:50.150
So while it's true that,
ultimately, the disclosure

00:23:50.150 --> 00:23:53.770
happened a few days before
we had originally intended,

00:23:53.770 --> 00:23:55.970
that's really almost
after the fact

00:23:55.970 --> 00:23:59.900
relative to the discussions
of how about that time was

00:23:59.900 --> 00:24:00.530
being managed.

00:24:00.530 --> 00:24:02.946
MATT LINTON: Yeah, so part of
that decision-making process

00:24:02.946 --> 00:24:06.230
here, in terms of
disclosure, is there

00:24:06.230 --> 00:24:09.080
is a really difficult and
constantly-evolving decision

00:24:09.080 --> 00:24:12.800
to be made around, is keeping
this issue on the down low

00:24:12.800 --> 00:24:16.790
and giving people time to fix
it better for users and better

00:24:16.790 --> 00:24:19.880
for individuals than
telling the world about it?

00:24:19.880 --> 00:24:21.545
And in the early
stages of this in,

00:24:21.545 --> 00:24:23.210
and in a lot of the
mitigation phases

00:24:23.210 --> 00:24:25.592
where everyone had
to develop patches,

00:24:25.592 --> 00:24:27.050
we had to performance
test patches,

00:24:27.050 --> 00:24:29.719
we had to validate that
they worked, obviously,

00:24:29.719 --> 00:24:31.760
keeping it on the down
low and giving people time

00:24:31.760 --> 00:24:35.240
to fix it so that, when the
world learned about it everyone

00:24:35.240 --> 00:24:37.550
would be prepared to
defend against it,

00:24:37.550 --> 00:24:38.900
was better for everyone.

00:24:38.900 --> 00:24:41.780
But to a certain
point, people began

00:24:41.780 --> 00:24:43.679
to realize that there
was a lot of fixing

00:24:43.679 --> 00:24:45.470
going on without a lot
of talking about it.

00:24:45.470 --> 00:24:47.660
And they began to put
two and two together.

00:24:47.660 --> 00:24:52.310
And very close to the end of
when we were almost prepared

00:24:52.310 --> 00:24:53.990
to announce it anyway,
some very clever

00:24:53.990 --> 00:24:57.950
people started putting together
public examples of both

00:24:57.950 --> 00:25:01.730
the flaw and how you might
use it to attack user data.

00:25:01.730 --> 00:25:04.700
And at the point where there's
public examples of someone

00:25:04.700 --> 00:25:06.650
being able to
exploit and attack,

00:25:06.650 --> 00:25:07.890
that equation is flipped.

00:25:07.890 --> 00:25:11.310
It's no longer better for
users and better for people

00:25:11.310 --> 00:25:14.160
to have a secret
vulnerability that they

00:25:14.160 --> 00:25:16.410
don't know how to defend
against if someone else knows

00:25:16.410 --> 00:25:17.502
how to attack.

00:25:17.502 --> 00:25:18.960
And so that kind
of forced our hand

00:25:18.960 --> 00:25:21.840
into talking about the issue
a little bit earlier than we

00:25:21.840 --> 00:25:23.160
were originally planning to.

00:25:23.160 --> 00:25:23.700
MELANIE WARRICK:
But you guys are

00:25:23.700 --> 00:25:25.074
pretty close to
getting out there

00:25:25.074 --> 00:25:26.440
anyways with this information.

00:25:26.440 --> 00:25:27.630
MATT LINTON: So close.

00:25:27.630 --> 00:25:29.505
PAUL TURNER: I think
the two important things

00:25:29.505 --> 00:25:32.550
to be aware of there, it really
was only a matter of days

00:25:32.550 --> 00:25:34.500
before the original
disclosure date.

00:25:34.500 --> 00:25:36.870
And then the other
thing, calling back

00:25:36.870 --> 00:25:39.840
to some of Matt's other
comments, the things

00:25:39.840 --> 00:25:42.360
that people were seeing
that were leading them

00:25:42.360 --> 00:25:46.590
to ask these questions and start
kind of probing these areas

00:25:46.590 --> 00:25:49.440
were those supply chain
level mitigations.

00:25:49.440 --> 00:25:52.020
They'd seen Linux deploying
patches from the up down.

00:25:52.020 --> 00:25:54.630
They'd seen Microsoft deploying
patches from the up down.

00:25:54.630 --> 00:25:56.690
Apple deployed
patches from up down.

00:25:56.690 --> 00:25:59.880
This kind of a
coordinated improvement

00:25:59.880 --> 00:26:01.470
doesn't happen by accident.

00:26:01.470 --> 00:26:03.900
So that kind of was immediately
setting off people's alarm

00:26:03.900 --> 00:26:06.280
bells that something
was going on.

00:26:06.280 --> 00:26:09.450
But also, the fact that
all of those vendors

00:26:09.450 --> 00:26:11.900
had the time to
develop those patches

00:26:11.900 --> 00:26:14.250
was why the disclosure
was delayed.

00:26:14.250 --> 00:26:16.500
So that, even though
people were seeing

00:26:16.500 --> 00:26:18.200
those things happen
in the supply chain,

00:26:18.200 --> 00:26:19.290
they had happened.

00:26:19.290 --> 00:26:22.380
So that when
disclosure did occur,

00:26:22.380 --> 00:26:24.510
even though it was
actually a few days early,

00:26:24.510 --> 00:26:27.070
there were protections
available-- and in many cases,

00:26:27.070 --> 00:26:28.200
deployed--

00:26:28.200 --> 00:26:30.780
to all of the major
computing platforms

00:26:30.780 --> 00:26:32.162
that users depended on.

00:26:32.162 --> 00:26:33.870
MARK MANDEL: Well, I
think we're slightly

00:26:33.870 --> 00:26:35.140
running out of time here.

00:26:35.140 --> 00:26:37.514
But before we wrap
up, is there anything

00:26:37.514 --> 00:26:39.930
that you want to make sure you
mention, maybe something we

00:26:39.930 --> 00:26:42.388
haven't covered, or if there's
extra information that maybe

00:26:42.388 --> 00:26:44.700
people should go look at,
or listen to, or watch

00:26:44.700 --> 00:26:46.950
that we haven't managed
to cover so far?

00:26:46.950 --> 00:26:47.700
PAUL TURNER: Sure.

00:26:47.700 --> 00:26:49.980
I think, generally
speaking, this

00:26:49.980 --> 00:26:51.510
was a really good
example of a lot

00:26:51.510 --> 00:26:53.551
of people in the industry
coming together to look

00:26:53.551 --> 00:26:55.200
at a really tough problem.

00:26:55.200 --> 00:26:59.070
And I expect that,
really, in many ways,

00:26:59.070 --> 00:27:00.740
we may have opened
Pandora's box here.

00:27:00.740 --> 00:27:03.850
There are going to be new
ways to attack these hardware

00:27:03.850 --> 00:27:04.544
features.

00:27:04.544 --> 00:27:06.210
People are really
going to start looking

00:27:06.210 --> 00:27:09.300
at other parts of the CPU and
how they could potentially be

00:27:09.300 --> 00:27:11.070
exploited in a similar fashion.

00:27:11.070 --> 00:27:15.300
And while that, obviously,
potentially presents

00:27:15.300 --> 00:27:19.800
a rich new buoyant vein of
exploits for malicious users,

00:27:19.800 --> 00:27:22.780
I'm really encouraged by the
fact that, as an industry,

00:27:22.780 --> 00:27:25.320
we seem to be moving
in a good direction

00:27:25.320 --> 00:27:28.125
to be able to prevent
them and protect users

00:27:28.125 --> 00:27:29.250
to the best of our ability.

00:27:29.250 --> 00:27:30.958
MATT LINTON: Yeah, I
don't think I really

00:27:30.958 --> 00:27:32.880
have too much to add on
top of what Paul said.

00:27:32.880 --> 00:27:34.830
I think there's
a lot of research

00:27:34.830 --> 00:27:37.170
in this area that's going to
go forward into the future.

00:27:37.170 --> 00:27:42.210
And I think, like it or not, the
next 10 years of the industry

00:27:42.210 --> 00:27:46.800
is still going to be a
constant cycle of finding out

00:27:46.800 --> 00:27:48.780
new and interesting
things that are

00:27:48.780 --> 00:27:51.812
unexpected about our software
and then rolling out ways

00:27:51.812 --> 00:27:53.520
to make them behave
the way we originally

00:27:53.520 --> 00:27:54.603
thought they would behave.

00:27:54.603 --> 00:27:58.650
And if that's not
an integral part

00:27:58.650 --> 00:28:01.252
of the computer industry at this
point, I don't know what is.

00:28:01.252 --> 00:28:02.460
MELANIE WARRICK: Well, great.

00:28:02.460 --> 00:28:04.860
Thank you both very much
for joining us and sharing

00:28:04.860 --> 00:28:05.640
this information.

00:28:05.640 --> 00:28:06.560
We appreciate it.

00:28:06.560 --> 00:28:07.880
And that's all we got.

00:28:07.880 --> 00:28:08.760
Thank you.

00:28:08.760 --> 00:28:09.210
MARK MANDEL: Thanks, guys.

00:28:09.210 --> 00:28:09.930
MATT LINTON: Yeah, thank you.

00:28:09.930 --> 00:28:10.470
Have a good one.

00:28:10.470 --> 00:28:11.370
MARK MANDEL: Thanks.

00:28:11.370 --> 00:28:12.780
MELANIE WARRICK: All right,
thanks, Matt and Paul.

00:28:12.780 --> 00:28:13.860
We appreciate that.

00:28:13.860 --> 00:28:17.250
And Mark, I think that's going
to be it for today's episode.

00:28:17.250 --> 00:28:19.405
Because we're keeping
it a special episode--

00:28:19.405 --> 00:28:19.800
MARK MANDEL: Short.

00:28:19.800 --> 00:28:21.750
MELANIE WARRICK: But we'll
definitely do our questions

00:28:21.750 --> 00:28:23.260
and cool things
for the next one.

00:28:23.260 --> 00:28:23.730
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:28:23.730 --> 00:28:24.628
See you on Wednesday then.

00:28:24.628 --> 00:28:25.878
MELANIE WARRICK: See you then.

